  <h2 id="problem"><span class="num">01</span>Problem statement</h2>
  <p>LinkedIn Premium's Career tier ($39.99/mo) is positioned as the upgrade that helps job seekers stand out, reach recruiters, and land interviews faster. In practice, user reviews across G2, Reddit, Quora, and independent blog analyses consistently report that the tier under-delivers on its core promise: Premium subscribers see low InMail response rates, dismiss the "Top Applicant" badge as meaningless, and frequently cancel after one or two months of paid use without seeing measurable job-search outcomes.</p>

  <h2 id="who"><span class="num">02</span>Who is facing this problem</h2>
  <p>All job seekers using LinkedIn as a primary channel for finding work — particularly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Active job seekers who upgrade to Premium specifically to accelerate their search</li>
    <li>Career changers needing visibility outside their existing network</li>
    <li>Early-career and student users for whom the $39.99/mo price is most burdensome</li>
    <li>Tech professionals in the US, where LinkedIn is the dominant platform for sourcing and applying to roles</li>
    <li>Passive candidates who pay for Premium hoping to be discovered by recruiters</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="why"><span class="num">03</span>Why it needs to be solved</h2>
  <p>The recruiting process is broken on both sides: candidates send applications into black holes, and recruiters are overwhelmed with low-signal inbound. Despite this, LinkedIn remains the platform US tech job seekers default to — there is no credible alternative at comparable scale. That dominance gives LinkedIn both the obligation and the opportunity to fix the value gap for the users who pay for it.</p>
  <p>The business case is also material:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Premium is LinkedIn's fastest-growing subscription product, generating <strong>$6.44B in 2023 revenue</strong></li>
    <li>Premium sign-ups grew <strong>+51% in FY2024</strong>, driven largely by new AI features</li>
    <li>Subscriber base reached <strong>~180M in 2024</strong> (up from 140M in 2022)</li>
    <li>With trial-to-paid conversion estimated at ~26% and monthly churn at ~19% over the first three months, even small lifts in perceived value translate to material revenue impact</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Failing to close the value gap risks ceding the job-seeker segment to focused competitors (Indeed, Welcome to the Jungle, Otta) and erodes trust in the broader LinkedIn brand.</p>

  <h2 id="pains"><span class="num">04</span>Pain point inventory</h2>
