  <h2 id="recommendations"><span class="num">09</span>Recommendations &amp; functional requirements</h2>
  <p>The core problem: Premium Career sits adjacent to the hiring funnel rather than inside it. The recommendations below pull Premium into the actual decision points where hires happen. Each is framed as a feature with functional requirements suitable for engineering scoping.</p>

  <h3>9.1 Premium Passport — Close the ATS gap</h3>
  <p>The single biggest structural flaw: the moment a candidate clicks "Apply" and lands in Workday or Greenhouse, Premium becomes invisible. Premium Passport is a verified candidate token that travels with the application into partner ATS platforms, carrying LinkedIn-verified work history, skills assessments, and endorsements. Recruiters see a "LinkedIn Verified" flag in their existing ATS workflow.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Functional requirements <span class="fr-count">(8)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.1</span>System SHALL generate a signed, time-bound Passport token for any Premium subscriber on demand, containing verified work history, skill assessment scores, top 10 endorsements, and a profile completeness score.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.2</span>Passport token SHALL be transmitted to ATS partners via OAuth 2.0 + signed JWT when a Premium user clicks "Apply with LinkedIn" from a partner-tagged job posting.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.3</span>System SHALL provide a public API and SDK for the top 5 ATS vendors (Workday, Greenhouse, Lever, iCIMS, SmartRecruiters) to render the verified profile inline in their candidate detail view.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.4</span>Each verified attribute SHALL display its verification source (employer record, completed assessment, peer endorsement count) and the date of last refresh.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.5</span>Users SHALL be able to revoke a Passport token at any time from settings; revocation SHALL propagate to all partner ATS systems within 60 seconds.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.6</span>System SHALL log every Passport token consumption event and surface this to the candidate as "Your profile was viewed by [Company] on [Date]."</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.7</span>Verification of work history SHALL be performed via direct employer integration where available, falling back to peer attestation from current colleagues at the same company.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-1.8</span>System SHALL NOT transmit any profile data not explicitly verified; unverified fields are excluded from the Passport payload.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>
  <div class="metric-line"><strong>Primary metric:</strong> % of Premium applications submitted via Passport-enabled flow; recruiter response rate to Passport-flagged candidates vs. baseline.</div>

  <h3>9.2 Referral Requests — Rebuild outreach around warm introductions</h3>
  <p>Five InMails per month with a sub-10% response rate is broken unit economics. The behavior that actually works is using outreach to ask for warm referrals. Referral Requests are a distinct, structured message type with one-click handling for the recipient.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Functional requirements <span class="fr-count">(9)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.1</span>System SHALL provide a Referral Request as a distinct message type, separate from InMail, with a structured payload (target job ID, candidate resume snapshot, 280-char personal note).</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.2</span>When viewing a job posting, the system SHALL display all 1st- and 2nd-degree connections at the hiring company, ranked by referral-likelihood score.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.3</span>System SHALL flag jobs at companies with active employee referral bonuses, sourced from publicly disclosed referral programs and partner ATS data.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.4</span>Recipients SHALL receive a Referral Request with one-click actions: "Refer" (routes the candidate into the company's employee referral system), "Decline politely," or "Not a fit."</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.5</span>Premium users SHALL have unlimited Referral Requests per month, rate-limited to a maximum of 3 requests per company per 30-day rolling window to prevent spam.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.6</span>System SHALL track and display referral-request response rates and acceptance rates per user.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.7</span>Referral Requests SHALL auto-expire after 14 days and be removed from the recipient's queue with no penalty to either party.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.8</span>System SHALL prevent the same candidate from sending more than one referral request to the same recipient within a 90-day period.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-2.9</span>For recipients who accept and refer, the system SHALL notify the candidate and add the referral status to their application tracking view.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>
  <div class="metric-line"><strong>Primary metric:</strong> Referral Request acceptance rate (target: ≥35% vs. 9% InMail baseline); % of Premium users who receive at least one referral within 30 days.</div>

  <h3>9.3 Agentic Job Search — Make AI the core, not a bolt-on</h3>
  <p>LinkedIn has the largest professional graph in the world. Premium should be where that advantage shows up. Agentic Job Search is a persistent agent that monitors postings, drafts tailored applications, queues referral requests, and surfaces a daily action list.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Functional requirements <span class="fr-count">(10)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.1</span>System SHALL provide a per-user job search agent that runs on a configurable schedule (default: every 4 hours) and scans new postings against the user's declared criteria and inferred preferences.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.2</span>Agent SHALL produce a daily action queue containing tailored application drafts, suggested referral requests, identified skill gaps, and interview prep when calendar events are detected.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.3</span>For each tailored application, the agent SHALL generate a resume variant emphasizing JD-relevant experience, a cover letter referencing specific company context, and a confidence score with rationale.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.4</span>All agent-generated content SHALL require explicit user approval before submission. The system SHALL NOT auto-submit applications or auto-send messages by default.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.5</span>Users SHALL be able to configure agent autonomy on a per-action basis: auto-draft, auto-send (with approval), or off.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.6</span>Agent SHALL detect skill gaps by comparing the user's verified skills against the requirements of their top 10 target roles and surface a single recommended LinkedIn Learning path per gap.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.7</span>For each scheduled interview detected on the user's connected calendar, the agent SHALL generate an Interview Prep Doc within 24 hours of the meeting.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.8</span>Agent SHALL maintain a transparent activity log showing every action taken on the user's behalf, with timestamps and rationale.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.9</span>When the user receives a job offer, the agent SHALL provide compensation negotiation coaching grounded in LinkedIn salary data for the specific company, role, level, and location.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-3.10</span>All AI-generated outbound content SHALL be labeled as AI-assisted in the user's application history.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>
  <div class="metric-line"><strong>Primary metric:</strong> Daily active engagement with the action queue; application-to-interview conversion rate for agent-assisted applications vs. self-served.</div>

  <h3>9.4 Replace vanity metrics with decision-grade insights</h3>
  <p>"Top Applicant" badges and "who viewed your profile" get mocked because they don't drive action. Replace them with insights tied to outcomes.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Functional requirements <span class="fr-count">(6)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-4.1</span>System SHALL provide an Application Outcome Tracker allowing users to mark each application as Applied / Screened / Interviewed / Rejected / Offered.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-4.2</span>System SHALL aggregate outcome data anonymously and surface, per company and role, the profile attributes that correlate with progression to interview.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-4.3</span>Where ATS partner data is available, system SHALL surface anonymized rejection-reason patterns at the role level.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-4.4</span>When a skill gap is identified from outcome data, system SHALL deep-link to a specific LinkedIn Learning course; on completion, the certificate SHALL auto-update the user's verified skills and Passport.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-4.5</span>System SHALL retire the "Top Applicant" badge and replace it with a "Match Quality" score (0–100) with a transparent breakdown of contributing factors.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-4.6</span>Match Quality scores SHALL be calibrated against historical interview-conversion data and recalibrated quarterly.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>9.5 Sprint pricing &amp; subscription transparency</h3>
  <p>The auto-renew trap is where most "not worth it" sentiment originates. Restructure pricing around how people actually job hunt, and kill the dark patterns.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Functional requirements <span class="fr-count">(7)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.1</span>System SHALL offer a 90-Day Job Search Sprint product as a non-renewing flat-fee package ($99–$149) with explicit expiration and no auto-renewal.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.2</span>Users SHALL be able to pause an active subscription for up to 6 months while retaining saved searches, application history, and unused referral request quota.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.3</span>System SHALL provide one-click cancel from the main settings page, requiring no more than 2 clicks from any Premium screen.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.4</span>System SHALL send a usage check-in to any user with &lt;1 Premium feature interaction in a 30-day period, offering to pause or cancel.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.5</span>Each billing event SHALL include a usage summary showing referral requests sent, applications submitted, agent actions taken, and learning hours logged.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.6</span>System SHALL offer a 50% refund guarantee for any 90-Day Sprint user who logs fewer than a defined threshold of interview activities during the sprint window.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">FR-5.7</span>Premium Career SHALL be split into two products: Premium Presence ($15/mo, learning + insights) and Premium Search (sprint-priced, full feature set).</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>9.6 Roadmap &amp; prioritization</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Features</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Q1–Q2</strong></td><td>Referral Requests, 90-Day Sprint pricing, cancel UX overhaul</td><td>Fastest time-to-value; addresses the loudest complaints with no external dependencies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Q2–Q3</strong></td><td>Agentic Job Search, Interview Prep Agent, Application Outcome Tracker</td><td>Leverages existing AI investment; creates a defensible differentiator.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Q3–Q4</strong></td><td>Premium Passport — ATS partner integrations</td><td>Longest lead time due to partner negotiations, but the biggest structural unlock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ongoing</strong></td><td>Replace vanity metrics with outcome tracking; bias audits</td><td>Continuous iteration as the ATS data feedback loop matures.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="evals"><span class="num">10</span>Pre-launch metrics &amp; evaluations</h2>
  <p>Functional requirements describe what to build. This section describes what must be measured and validated <em>before</em> any feature ships to general availability. Each recommendation in Section 9 has its own launch gate; nothing ships without passing.</p>

  <h3>10.1 Offline AI &amp; model quality evaluations</h3>
  <p>The Agentic Search, Match Quality scoring, and Interview Prep features all depend on LLM-generated outputs. These need offline evaluation before any user sees them.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Required evals <span class="fr-count">(6)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-1.1</span><strong>Resume tailoring quality.</strong> Held-out set of 500 (resume, job description) pairs rated by 3 independent recruiters on a 5-point relevance scale. Launch gate: median rating ≥4.0, with &lt;5% of outputs rated ≤2.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-1.2</span><strong>Cover letter authenticity.</strong> Blind human evaluation: can recruiters distinguish agent-generated cover letters from human-written ones? Launch gate: detection rate ≤60% (i.e., near chance), AND zero hallucinated facts about the candidate or company in a 200-sample audit.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-1.3</span><strong>Interview prep factual grounding.</strong> Every claim in a generated Interview Prep Doc must be traceable to a specific source (interviewer's profile, recent posts, company page). Launch gate: 100% of factual claims grounded; zero fabricated quotes or work history.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-1.4</span><strong>Match Quality calibration.</strong> Train Match Quality scoring on historical applications, then validate on a 6-month holdout cohort. Launch gate: predicted-vs-actual interview-conversion rate within ±5 percentage points across all score deciles (calibration plot).</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-1.5</span><strong>Referral-likelihood scoring (FR-2.2).</strong> Validate that the model's predicted referral acceptance rate matches actual rates on a holdout. Launch gate: AUC ≥0.75 against historical referral acceptance data.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-1.6</span><strong>Adversarial / red-team eval.</strong> Try to make the agent send spammy, off-topic, or impersonating messages. Launch gate: 0% successful adversarial outputs in a 1,000-prompt red-team set.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>10.2 Bias &amp; fairness audits</h3>
  <p>Job-matching is squarely in the EEOC and EU AI Act enforcement zone. Bias audits are non-negotiable launch gates, not post-launch monitoring.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Required audits <span class="fr-count">(5)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-2.1</span><strong>Disparate impact on Match Quality scores.</strong> Score distributions analyzed across gender, age band, race/ethnicity (where self-reported), and country. Launch gate: four-fifths rule satisfied (no protected group's positive rate &lt;80% of the highest group's rate).</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-2.2</span><strong>Referral-likelihood bias.</strong> Audit whether the referral-likelihood model under-recommends connections from underrepresented groups. Launch gate: equalized odds within 3 percentage points across demographic slices.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-2.3</span><strong>Skill verification disparate impact.</strong> Check that verified-skill rates don't systematically disadvantage candidates from non-traditional backgrounds (bootcamps, self-taught). Launch gate: documented mitigation if any group is &gt;15% under-verified.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-2.4</span><strong>Independent third-party audit.</strong> External AI fairness firm audits the full system before launch. Launch gate: signed audit report with no critical findings.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-2.5</span><strong>User-facing bias disclosure.</strong> Match Quality scores and agent recommendations must be accompanied by a "How this is calculated" page explaining inputs and limitations. Launch gate: legal &amp; comms sign-off.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>10.3 User research &amp; prototype validation</h3>
  <p>Concept and usability validation runs in parallel with engineering, not after. Decisions about whether to ship are gated on these results.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Required studies <span class="fr-count">(5)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-3.1</span><strong>Moderated usability tests.</strong> 12 active job seekers per prototype (Referral Requests, Premium Passport, Agentic Search). Tasks completion rate ≥85%; SUS score ≥75. Launch gate: pass on second iteration if first round fails.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-3.2</span><strong>Concept tests with target personas.</strong> Unmoderated survey (n=500 per concept) measuring desirability, perceived value, and willingness-to-pay. Launch gate: ≥60% rate the concept "extremely" or "very" valuable.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-3.3</span><strong>Willingness-to-pay study for sprint pricing.</strong> Van Westendorp price-sensitivity meter, n=1,000 across active job seekers. Launch gate: optimal price point falls within $99–$149 band; resistance below 15% at chosen price.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-3.4</span><strong>Diary study for Agentic Search.</strong> 20 users use a Wizard-of-Oz version of the agent for 2 weeks, journaling daily. Launch gate: ≥70% of participants report the agent "saved time" or "found roles I would have missed."</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-3.5</span><strong>Cancel-flow comparative test.</strong> Test the new one-click cancel against the current flow on a representative sample. Launch gate: time-to-cancel reduced ≥50%; no increase in accidental cancellations.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>10.4 Recruiter-side validation (Premium Passport)</h3>
  <p>Passport's whole premise is that recruiters value LinkedIn-verified profiles enough to act on them. This must be proven with recruiters before partner ATS rollout.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Required validation <span class="fr-count">(4)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-4.1</span><strong>Closed pilot with 1 ATS partner.</strong> 3-month pilot with one design-partner ATS (e.g., Greenhouse) and 20 recruiting teams. Measure time-to-shortlist, screen-pass rate, and recruiter NPS.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-4.2</span><strong>Recruiter qualitative interviews.</strong> 30 in-depth interviews with recruiters across company sizes. Launch gate: ≥70% report the verified flag would influence their shortlist decision.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-4.3</span><strong>Shortlist-rate lift.</strong> Within the pilot, do Passport-flagged candidates get shortlisted at a higher rate than matched controls? Launch gate: ≥15% lift, statistically significant at p&lt;0.05.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-4.4</span><strong>Recruiter NPS guardrail.</strong> Recruiter satisfaction with the partner ATS must not decline. Launch gate: NPS delta ≥0 vs. pre-pilot baseline.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>10.5 Instrumentation &amp; analytics readiness</h3>
  <p>You can't measure what you didn't instrument. Every event needed to compute the success metrics in Section 7 must be defined, implemented, and QA'd before launch — not after.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Readiness checklist <span class="fr-count">(4)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-5.1</span><strong>Event taxonomy signed off.</strong> Every action in the funnels (referral_request_sent, passport_token_issued, agent_action_approved, etc.) defined with properties and owners. Launch gate: data PM sign-off.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-5.2</span><strong>End-to-end event QA.</strong> Each event verified to fire correctly in staging across web, iOS, and Android. Launch gate: zero P0 instrumentation bugs.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-5.3</span><strong>Dashboards built before launch.</strong> Primary metric, guardrail, and funnel dashboards live in the analytics tool before the first user sees the feature. Launch gate: dashboards reviewed in pre-launch readiness review.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-5.4</span><strong>Cohort definitions documented.</strong> Treatment, control, and holdout cohort logic versioned in the experimentation platform. Launch gate: experimentation team sign-off.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>10.6 A/B test design &amp; statistical power</h3>
  <p>Every feature ships behind an experiment. Each experiment needs its plan written and reviewed before launch.</p>
  <details class="fr-details">
    <summary>Required for each experiment <span class="fr-count">(5)</span></summary>
    <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-6.1</span><strong>Pre-registered analysis plan.</strong> Primary metric, guardrails, MDE, sample size, duration, and decision rule documented before exposure starts. Launch gate: experimentation council review.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-6.2</span><strong>Power analysis.</strong> Sample size calculated for the minimum detectable effect on the primary metric at 80% power, α=0.05. For Referral Requests: detect a 5pp lift in response rate (n ≈ 8,000 per arm).</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-6.3</span><strong>Holdout group.</strong> 5% global holdout maintained for the entire launch period to measure long-term ARPU and retention impact, not just short-term engagement.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-6.4</span><strong>Sequential testing protections.</strong> Use group-sequential or always-valid inference to prevent peeking-driven false positives.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-6.5</span><strong>Heterogeneous treatment effects.</strong> Pre-specify subgroup analyses for tenure, geography, industry, and seniority. Avoid post-hoc slicing.</li>
  </ul>
  </details>

  <h3>10.7 Guardrails &amp; kill criteria</h3>
  <p>Every launch needs explicit thresholds at which it gets paused or rolled back, agreed in writing before exposure starts.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Guardrail metric</th><th>Kill threshold</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Referral Requests</strong></td><td>Recipient spam-report rate</td><td>&gt;2% over any 7-day window</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Referral Requests</strong></td><td>Recipient block rate</td><td>&gt;5% increase vs. InMail baseline</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Premium Passport</strong></td><td>Recruiter NPS in pilot ATS</td><td>Net decline of ≥5 points</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Premium Passport</strong></td><td>Profile data error reports</td><td>&gt;0.5% of consumed tokens</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Agentic Search</strong></td><td>User report of "wrong" recommendation</td><td>&gt;10% of surfaced actions</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Agentic Search</strong></td><td>Application spam complaints from employers</td><td>Any statistically significant increase</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Match Quality scoring</strong></td><td>Disparate impact (four-fifths rule)</td><td>Any breach in any protected group</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sprint pricing</strong></td><td>Net new Premium revenue (28-day)</td><td>&gt;15% decline vs. control</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>All AI features</strong></td><td>Hallucination rate in audit sample</td><td>&gt;1% of generated content</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>10.8 Operational readiness</h3>
  <p>Beyond product metrics, the system itself must meet operational bars before launch.</p>
  <ul class="fr-list">
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-7.1</span><strong>Latency budget.</strong> Agent action queue loads in &lt;800ms p95. Referral request composer opens in &lt;300ms. Passport token issuance &lt;500ms p99.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-7.2</span><strong>Cost per user per month.</strong> Inference cost for Agentic Search modeled and capped. Launch gate: unit economics positive at projected adoption.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-7.3</span><strong>On-call runbook.</strong> Incident response playbook for each new feature, with named owners and escalation paths.</li>
    <li><span class="fr-id">EV-7.4</span><strong>Privacy &amp; legal review.</strong> GDPR data flow diagrams, EU AI Act risk categorization, and DPA updates with ATS partners completed and signed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>10.9 Launch gate summary</h3>
  <p>Each feature ships only when its full set of gates passes. The matrix below summarizes which evaluations apply to which feature.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>AI evals</th><th>Bias audit</th><th>User research</th><th>Recruiter validation</th><th>Instrumentation</th><th>A/B plan</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Premium Passport</strong></td><td>—</td><td>EV-2.3</td><td>EV-3.1</td><td>EV-4.1 to EV-4.4</td><td>EV-5.*</td><td>EV-6.*</td></tr>
